      "claim": "The formal sources of international law are listed in Article 38(1) of the ICJ Statute: treaties, customary international law, and general principles of law.",
      "evidence": "Article 38(1) ICJ Statute enumerates these sources.",
      "weight": 0.3,
      "depends_on": []
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "claim": "Article 38(1) does not prescribe any hierarchy among these sources.",
      "evidence": "The text simply lists the sources without ranking; customary practice and scholarship confirm there is no formal hierarchy.",
      "weight": 0.4,
      "depends_on": [1]
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "claim": "In case of conflict, principles such as lex specialis or lex posterior may apply, but they are rules of interpretation, not a hierarchy of sources.",
      "evidence": "International law doctrine treats conflicts by applying interpretive rules, not by stating that one source outranks another.",
      "weight": 0.15,
      "depends_on": [2]
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "claim": "Option A states there is no hierarchy between the formal sources, matching the doctrinal position.",
      "evidence": "Steps 1‑3 establish the lack of hierarchy.",
      "weight": 0.1,
      "depends_on": [2]
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "claim": "Options B, C, and D each assert a hierarchy that is not supported by the primary source (Article 38).",
      "evidence": "No provision in Article 38 or customary practice gives treaties, custom, or general principles supremacy over the others.",
